From 61dd67b1f426e1e858267753c2bd353fdb7cc109 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kryxzort <101042137+Kryxzort@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 23:26:53 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 131/139] render: upload a wallpaper's JPEG / PNG textures on a
 context of the host's while it builds

A wallpaper's JPEG / PNG textures decode on the pool's threads while it builds (0128), but their pixels still
reached the GPU in finishTextureUploads, on the render thread, after the build. NVIDIA converts a texture's pixels
on the CPU of the thread that calls glTexImage2D: measured on the scene worker (thread CPU time around each
call), 333 MB of 3163060610's 152 uploads took 202 ms of CPU, 1.6 GB/s; with nothing passed (glTexImage2D(NULL))
the same calls took 1 ms. Uploading from a pixel buffer object made it slower (536 ms; 334 ms with GL_STATIC_DRAW:
the driver reads the buffer back on the CPU), and BGRA source pixels changed nothing. So the work itself can't
shrink here; it can only leave the render thread.

wp_context_set_gl_upload_context gives the core a second context in the share group of the host's: a
make_current callback the core's upload thread (wp-tex-upload, one per context, stopped in wp_context_destroy
after the texture cache) calls once, and release as it stops. The thread starts once a context's deferred images
reach 64 MB of RGBA8 (~40 ms of conversion); the images before that upload on the render thread as before. Started
at the first image, it cost more than it saved below that: a scene whose images convert in ~3 ms built ~20 ms
slower, scenes with 30-60 MB of images 0-50 ms slower (starting the thread, its context, its uploads beside the
render thread's own GL calls). A decode
job then uploads its pixels on that thread and waits for it, and the fence after the upload goes back with the
image; finishUpload binds the texture (the re-bind after which the render context sees it), glWaitSync's the fence
on the GPU and deletes it. Each decode job waits for its upload: with the jobs queuing uploads and going on to the
next decode, the heavy scenes gained less (3163060610 warm 516 -> 647 ms against the waiting version). Without the call, or when make_current fails, nothing changes: the pixels upload on the
render thread as before. A texture destroyed before finishUpload waits for its decode and upload first.

Measured on a private headless liascape-player (scene_load.py, apply to first frame, medians of 4 cold / 8 warm /
4 after-a-boot loads; this core with its Container patch and the worker giving it the context, against the pin,
alternating): 3042492564 warm 571 -> 480 ms, cold 1110 -> 945, boot 609 -> 527; 3163060610 warm 468 -> 426,
cold 808 -> 695, boot 513 -> 428; 2388299037 warm 470 -> 424; 2290002190 256 -> 249; 1864677589 290 -> 271;
2511663284 262 -> 273 (cold 432 -> 375); 3480481965 273 -> 268. Same pictures: captures 1 s after the first frame
differ from the pin's no more than two of the pin's own loads differ from each other.

Origin: ours
Why: docs/TODO.md (scene wallpapers load faster: texture uploads after the pool's decode)
Status: candidate-for-upstream
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
